# Bug, as M1 runs it. This is the real definition a running Fleet loads.
#
# There is a second `bug.json` in this repository, at
# `crates/core-model/domain/workflow-samples/bug.json`, and the two are not
# duplicates. Read this before reconciling them:
#
#   - **That one is the designed workflow, and it is correct.** Seven steps —
#     repro, root_cause, fix, regression_verify, review, merge, close — with
#     judge checks, evidence scopes, gate policy, and a `review` step that
#     routes `request_changes` back to `fix`. It declares `structure: "loop"`,
#     which is what that routing makes it. It is the authority on what Bug
#     becomes, and it lives with the registry that records that decision.
#
#   - **This one is M1's reduced form, and it is what runs today.** Three
#     steps, no Judge, no loop, `advance_gate: auto` throughout. Reducing it
#     is the only way to have a workflow that dispatches at all right now.
#     `implement` carries the test check itself rather than handing off to a
#     separate `verify` step — the Drone never runs a Check; Fleet does, after
#     the Drone reports its diff, and a step the Drone cannot advance past
#     until build and test both pass needs no second step to say so again.
#
#   - **The designed one does not load at M1, and that is not a defect in it.**
#     `crates/config` carries one of the two structures and one of the four
#     advance gates, because there is no Judge to answer
#     `auto_if_judge_passes` and no verdict to route a loop on. Its refusal is
#     `OutsideM1` — a value the schema sanctions that this milestone has not
#     built. The fix is a later milestone, not an edit to that file.
#
# So: do not reconcile them, and do not copy one over the other. When M1's
# gates grow a Judge and a loop, this file goes away and the designed
# definition is what a repository ships.
#
# A `#` comment is YAML, not JSON. `crates/config` reads both files with a YAML
# parser — JSON is a subset of YAML — so this loads, but a strict JSON reader
# will not take it. The extension is kept because the step names this path and
# because a repository's workflows are the shape `workflow-samples/` already
# established.
#
# Six fields, and no others. `version`, `workflow_id`, `name`, `structure`,
# `steps[]`, and within a step `id`, `label`, `evidence_type`,
# `mechanical_checks[]` and `advance_gate`. Everything else in the schema is
# refused by name.
#
# `workflow_id` is the key the designed definition beside this one already
# carried and M1 did not read. Without it nothing joined a proposal's
# `workflow_id` to a workflow, so a Job could be proposed against a workflow
# that does not exist, stored, and shown on the board claiming one Fleet has
# never heard of. The value is the slug, exactly as the designed file spells it.
{
  "version": 1,
  "workflow_id": "bug",
  "name": "bug",
  "structure": "linear",
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "plan",
      "label": "Plan the change",
      "evidence_type": "facts_note",
      "advance_gate": "auto"
    },
    {
      "id": "implement",
      "label": "Implement",
      "evidence_type": "diff",
      "mechanical_checks": [
        { "type": "manifest_check", "check": "build", "expect_exit_code": 0 },
        { "type": "manifest_check", "check": "test", "expect_exit_code": 0 },
        { "type": "diff_nonempty" }
      ],
      "advance_gate": "auto"
    },
    {
      "id": "handoff",
      "label": "Summarise",
      "evidence_type": "facts_note",
      "advance_gate": "auto"
    }
  ]
}
